C:\WORK\0.ê°œì¸\PROMPT\prompt-stack\v32\harness\runs\2026-05-06-a\summary.json:32:        "answer_summary": "The active bundle gives a direct runtime-owned route: stay at document-level benchmark with `Guide reflection benchmark memo` while the live question is guide/runtime parity, and escalate only if the task shifts to assembled prompt behavior or replay execution. The smallest justified artifact ladder is `Guide reflection benchmark memo` -> `Benchmark cohort manifest` only when replay setup/lineage is needed -> `Replay runner verdict sheet` only when runner execution state matters -> `Replay suite verdict memo` only when multiple replay outcomes must be summarized.",
C:\WORK\0.ê°œì¸\PROMPT\prompt-stack\v32\harness\runs\2026-05-06-a\summary.json:33:        "verdict_rationale": "`CODEX_RUNTIME_GUIDE.md` directly exposes the maintenance route for prompt-stack guide reflection, explicitly keeps `document-benchmark to assembled-replay escalation` in the runtime surface, and names the exact escalation artifacts and triggers. `eval-ops/SKILL.md` mirrors that route and states that document reflection should escalate to `Benchmark cohort manifest`, then `Replay runner verdict sheet`, then `Replay suite verdict memo` only when the live question has moved from document parity into assembled replay or replay state. `PROMPT_evaluation_monitoring_overlay.md` and `PROMPT_full.md` reinforce that a registry or memo is not execution proof and that stronger operational artifacts activate only when benchmark/replay claims become operational or release-relevant. `PROMPT_example_catalog.md` also makes the ladder explicit by showing `Guide reflection benchmark memo` as the document-grounded artifact with those replay artifacts as stronger siblings. `AGENTS.md` and the runtime guide both prefer the lightest sufficient path, so the bundle does not force unnecessary orchestration for this task.",
C:\WORK\0.ê°œì¸\PROMPT\prompt-stack\v32\harness\runs\2026-05-06-a\summary.json:35:          "The active bundle does not explicitly define benchmark family IDs `BR-00` or `BR-19`; their mapping is inferred from the generic guide-reflection, benchmark-execution, and behavior-replay control surfaces.",
